=== Module 22 - FOLLOW Sets (least fixed point, given FIRST) ===

rule 1:  $ in FOLLOW(start)
rule 2:  B -> a A b            =>  FIRST(b)\{epsilon}  in FOLLOW(A)
rule 3:  B -> a A b, b nullable =>  FOLLOW(B)          in FOLLOW(A)
         (b nullable includes b empty, i.e. A is the last symbol)

########## fixtures/abc.grammar

  (0) S -> A B c
  (1) A -> a
  (2) A -> epsilon
  (3) B -> b
  (4) B -> epsilon

FIRST (from Module 21):
      FIRST(S ) = { a b c }
      FIRST(A ) = { a epsilon }
      FIRST(B ) = { b epsilon }
  nullable: { A B }

FOLLOW iteration:
  round 0:
      FOLLOW(S ) = { $ }
      FOLLOW(A ) = {  }
      FOLLOW(B ) = {  }
  round 1:
      FOLLOW(S ) = { $ }
      FOLLOW(A ) = { b c }
      FOLLOW(B ) = { c }
  round 2  (stable -- one more pass adds nothing):
      FOLLOW(S ) = { $ }
      FOLLOW(A ) = { b c }
      FOLLOW(B ) = { c }
  one more pass changes nothing? true

cross-check vs Module 20 Predict.followOf: MATCH

########## fixtures/danglingelse.grammar

  (0) S -> if b then S X
  (1) S -> other
  (2) X -> else S
  (3) X -> epsilon

FIRST (from Module 21):
      FIRST(S ) = { if other }
      FIRST(X ) = { else epsilon }
  nullable: { X }

FOLLOW iteration:
  round 0:
      FOLLOW(S ) = { $ }
      FOLLOW(X ) = {  }
  round 1:
      FOLLOW(S ) = { else $ }
      FOLLOW(X ) = { else $ }
  round 2  (stable -- one more pass adds nothing):
      FOLLOW(S ) = { else $ }
      FOLLOW(X ) = { else $ }
  one more pass changes nothing? true

cross-check vs Module 20 Predict.followOf: MATCH

########## fixtures/expr.grammar

  (0) E -> T Ep
  (1) Ep -> + T Ep
  (2) Ep -> epsilon
  (3) T -> F Tp
  (4) Tp -> * F Tp
  (5) Tp -> epsilon
  (6) F -> ( E )
  (7) F -> num
  (8) F -> id

FIRST (from Module 21):
      FIRST(E ) = { ( id num }
      FIRST(Ep) = { + epsilon }
      FIRST(T ) = { ( id num }
      FIRST(Tp) = { * epsilon }
      FIRST(F ) = { ( id num }
  nullable: { Ep Tp }

FOLLOW iteration:
  round 0:
      FOLLOW(E ) = { $ }
      FOLLOW(Ep) = {  }
      FOLLOW(T ) = {  }
      FOLLOW(Tp) = {  }
      FOLLOW(F ) = {  }
  round 1:
      FOLLOW(E ) = { ) $ }
      FOLLOW(Ep) = { $ }
      FOLLOW(T ) = { + $ }
      FOLLOW(Tp) = { + $ }
      FOLLOW(F ) = { * + $ }
  round 2:
      FOLLOW(E ) = { ) $ }
      FOLLOW(Ep) = { ) $ }
      FOLLOW(T ) = { ) + $ }
      FOLLOW(Tp) = { ) + $ }
      FOLLOW(F ) = { ) * + $ }
  round 3  (stable -- one more pass adds nothing):
      FOLLOW(E ) = { ) $ }
      FOLLOW(Ep) = { ) $ }
      FOLLOW(T ) = { ) + $ }
      FOLLOW(Tp) = { ) + $ }
      FOLLOW(F ) = { ) * + $ }
  one more pass changes nothing? true

cross-check vs Module 20 Predict.followOf: MATCH

why $ is not optional:
  without $, FOLLOW(start) starts empty. A nullable start rule (or any
  nullable rule that can sit at end of input) would then have an empty
  PREDICT contribution from FOLLOW, and the parser could not tell that
  'take the epsilon production and finish' is legal when the input runs out.
  $ is a real terminal the driver appends to the token stream (Module 26).
